<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marne</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as River Marne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Marne is a river of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, about 500 km long, that rises on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a> plateau and flows through Champagne to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> just south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalons-en-champagne">Châlons-en-Champagne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meaux">Meaux</a> grew on its banks. It gave its name to two major battles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, in 1914 and 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marne.jpg" alt="Marne" width="320" height="167" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pline · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Marne_a_Dormans_DSC_0117.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
